# Report: dao.space.query — collection and relation binding forms for function-clause results

**Status:** done, with one check not run: `cljstyle check` was blocked by the permission gate. Everything else passes. The work is on top of master b9665935 in the main tree, nothing is staged or committed, and only the four allowed files changed.

## Result

Function-clause results now accept the same binding forms as `:in`, and they behave as in Datomic:
- scalar `?x` binds the whole result (unchanged);
- tuple `[?a ?b]` binds by position (unchanged, including its arity error);
- collection `[?x ...]` gives one binding per element;
- relation `[[?a ?b]]` gives one binding per tuple.

Inside these forms, terms are matched the same way pattern terms are:
- `_` matches anything and binds nothing;
- a bare symbol is a constant the element must equal;
- a variable that is already bound filters the elements.

An empty collection or relation gives no binding, so the row is filtered out. If a collection or relation form gets a result that isn't sequential (`42`, `:k`, `nil`, a map or a set), the query throws "…must be sequential". Malformed forms throw `Unsupported binding form — use ?x, [?a ?b], [?x ...] or [[?a ?b]]`.

The REPL example now works on every VM: after `(defn inc [i] (+ i 1))`, "all literal operands of yin/def" returns `#{[inc]}`.

## Implementation

- **`src/cljc/dao/space/query.cljc`:**
  - New `unify-tuple`, which is the old inline tuple logic pulled out so relation tuples can share it.
  - New `bind-fn-result`, called by the non-special-form path of `eval-fn-clause`.
  - The form is classified by the existing `classify-in-pattern`, so there is one grammar for binding forms.
  - I did not reuse `expand-in-binding`: it builds bindings with `zipmap`, which would overwrite a bound variable, turn `_` into a key, and bind bare symbols instead of comparing them.
- **`docs/design/dao.space.query.md`:** the "Predicates & function clauses" section now says results accept the same binding forms as `:in`. The old "collection/relation … not implemented" text is replaced by the actual errors.

